---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BlogPosting',
  headline: 'Pakka (0.91) vs Sunee (0.90) Ratti: The Definitive Gemstone Guide to Avoid Overpaying',
  description: 'Understand the critical difference between 0.91 Pakka Ratti (182 mg) and 0.90 Sunee Ratti (180 mg). Learn the math, financial losses, and certified testing standards.',
  author: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Ratti to Carat Research Team',
    url: 'https://rattitocarat.com'
  },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Ratti to Carat',
    logo: {
      '@type': 'ImageObject',
      url: 'https://rattitocarat.com/favicon.svg'
    }
  },
  datePublished: '2025-01-15',
  dateModified: '2025-01-15',
  image: 'https://rattitocarat.com/og-image.png',
  mainEntityOfPage: {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://rattitocarat.com/blog/pakka-vs-sunee-ratti'
  }
};
---

<Layout
  title="Pakka (0.91) vs Sunee (0.90) Ratti Guide | Ratti to Carat"
  description="Learn why the difference between 0.9100 Pakka Ratti (182 mg) and 0.9000 Sunee Ratti (180 mg) matters in gemstone buying, pricing, and lab certificates."
  canonicalUrl="https://rattitocarat.com/blog/pakka-vs-sunee-ratti"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} slot="head" />

  <Navbar />

  <main class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-10 space-y-10">
    <!-- Breadcrumbs -->
    <nav class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs text-slate-500 font-mono">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-amber-700 transition-colors">Home</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <a href="/blog" class="hover:text-amber-700 transition-colors">Blog</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span class="text-slate-800 font-semibold truncate">Pakka vs Sunee Ratti</span>
    </nav>

    <!-- Article Header -->
    <header class="space-y-4 text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <span class="px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-amber-50 text-amber-800 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider border border-amber-200">
          Gemstone Standards
        </span>
        <span class="text-slate-400">•</span>
        <span class="text-slate-500 font-mono">6 Min Read</span>
        <span class="text-slate-400">•</span>
        <span class="text-slate-500 font-mono">Updated Jan 2025</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900 font-display leading-tight">
        Pakka (0.91) vs Sunee (0.90) Ratti: The Definitive Guide to Avoid Overpaying
      </h1>

      <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        Why a tiny difference of 0.01 between 182 milligrams and 180 milligrams causes major financial loss when buying precious Vedic gems like Pukhraj, Neelam, and Panna.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Key Takeaway Box -->
    <div class="p-5 sm:p-6 rounded-3xl bg-amber-50/70 border border-amber-200/80 space-y-2 shadow-xs">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-bold text-amber-900 uppercase tracking-wide">
        <span>⚡ Quick Summary</span>
      </div>
      <ul class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-1.5 list-disc list-inside">
        <li><strong>Pakka Ratti (0.9100 Carats = 182.0 mg):</strong> The standard recognized by gemological testing laboratories and ethical jewelers.</li>
        <li><strong>Sunee Ratti (0.9000 Carats = 180.0 mg):</strong> An informal traditional trade convention that delivers ~1.1% less physical gem weight.</li>
        <li><strong>Rule of Thumb:</strong> Always ask your jeweler: <em>"Are your Ratti prices calculated at 182 mg or 180 mg?"</em></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Article Body -->
    <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none text-slate-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed space-y-8">
      
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 font-display">
          1. The Historical Origin of Ratti and the Gunja Seed
        </h2>
        <p>
          Centuries before digital analytical balances existed in India, ancient lapidaries and goldsmiths required an infallible, naturally consistent unit of mass. They turned to the wild scarlet seed of the <em>Abrus precatorius</em> plant, colloquially known across Northern India as the <strong>Gunja (गुंजा) or Ratti (रत्ती)</strong> seed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because wild mature Gunja seeds exhibit an astonishingly uniform average mass of approximately 1.82 Troy grains (~120–182 milligrams), ancient trade treatises like the <em>Arthashastra</em> codified the Ratti as India’s foundational unit of precious weight.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 font-display">
          2. The Mathematical Comparison: Pakka vs Sunee
        </h2>
        <p>
          When the international gem trade transitioned to the <strong>Metric Carat</strong> (formally standardized in 1907 at exactly 200 milligrams or 0.200 grams), Indian jewelers harmonized the traditional Ratti to metric units in two distinct ways:
        </p>

        <!-- Comparison Table -->
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 my-4">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono border-collapse">
            <thead class="bg-slate-50 text-slate-700 uppercase font-semibold border-b border-slate-200">
              <tr>
                <th class="p-3">Standard</th>
                <th class="p-3">Carats per Ratti</th>
                <th class="p-3">Milligrams (mg)</th>
                <th class="p-3">Status</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100 bg-white">
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-emerald-800">Pakka Ratti (पक्की रत्ती)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold">0.9100 Ct</td>
                <td class="p-3">182.0 mg</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-sans text-emerald-700 font-medium">Lab Standard (Certified)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-amber-800">Sunee Ratti (सुनी रत्ती)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold">0.9000 Ct</td>
                <td class="p-3">180.0 mg</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-sans text-amber-700 font-medium">Bazaar Custom (Informal)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 italic">
          Difference: Sunee Ratti delivers 2 milligrams less physical gem material per Ratti, creating a 1.0989% discrepancy.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 font-display">
          3. The Financial Impact: How Buyers Lose Money
        </h2>
        <p>
          A difference of 0.01 Carats might sound negligible on paper, but for high-grade astrological gemstones such as untreated Ceylon Yellow Sapphires (Pukhraj) or Kashmiri Blue Sapphires (Neelam), rates easily reach ₹25,000 to ₹100,000 per Ratti.
        </p>
        <p>
          Consider purchasing an astrologically recommended <strong>7.25 Ratti Yellow Sapphire</strong> at ₹30,000 per Ratti:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1.5 pl-2">
          <li>Under <strong>Pakka (0.91)</strong>, you must receive <code>7.25 × 0.91 = 6.5975 Carats</code> (1,319.5 mg).</li>
          <li>Under <strong>Sunee (0.90)</strong>, the jeweler only provides <code>7.25 × 0.90 = 6.5250 Carats</code> (1,305.0 mg).</li>
          <li>You are shortchanged by <strong>0.0725 Carats (14.5 milligrams)</strong> of untreated sapphire, which amounts to over <strong>₹2,400 in lost value</strong> on that single stone!</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 font-display">
          4. Why Laboratory Certificates Never Print "Ratti"
        </h2>
        <p>
          A common frustration among buyers is that international testing laboratories (such as GIA, IGI, GRS, and government test centers) will only certify weights in <strong>Carats (Ct)</strong> or grams.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because Ratti is a cultural and commercial trade denomination rather than a legally defined SI unit, ISO and CIBJO standards forbid laboratories from certifying in Ratti. Therefore, buyers must independently convert the certified Carat weight back to Ratti using the 0.9100 formula:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-100 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold text-slate-900">
          Pakka Ratti = Certificate Carat Weight ÷ 0.9100
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Bottom Interactive CTA -->
      <section class="p-6 rounded-2xl atelier-card-elevated border border-amber-300/80 bg-amber-50/50 space-y-3 text-center my-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 font-display">
          Verify Any Gemstone Certificate Instantly
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 max-w-md mx-auto">
          Use our certified bidirectional calculator to verify both Pakka (0.91) and Sunee (0.90) equivalents with milligram accuracy.
        </p>
        <div class="pt-2">
          <a
            href="/"
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 hover:bg-slate-800 text-white font-bold text-xs shadow-sm transition-all hover:scale-105"
          >
            <span>Open Master Calculator →</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
